{
  "result": "At the edge of my neighborhood stands a small library built from an old train station. Its clock has been stopped at four seventeen for years, yet nobody seems eager to repair it. Perhaps the town enjoys having one place where time refuses to hurry.\n\nInside, the shelves lean slightly, as if listening. A volunteer named Mara keeps a bowl of apples beside the returns desk. She says people make better choices about books when they are not hungry. I have no evidence for this theory, but I appreciate its kindness.\n\nLast autumn, a child found a pressed maple leaf inside a book about oceans. On its back, someone had written, \u201cRemember the day we got lost and found the sea.\u201d The child asked whether the message was part of the story. Mara thought for a moment and said that now it was.\n\nI think about that answer whenever I pass the station. Most places collect traces of people: scratches on tables, faded signs, names carved where they should not be. A library makes a gentler promise. It keeps those traces near other stories, giving strangers a chance to discover them.\n\nYesterday I returned a book on gardening and borrowed one about stars. Outside, rain had just ended. The clock still read four seventeen, while the evening moved quietly around it. I walked home with the comfort that a place need not be perfect, punctual, or grand to help us notice how much of life is worth keeping and sharing.",
